  "interpretation": {
    "observed_general_catalog_benefit": true,
    "evidence": "Catalog-full passed 9/9 holdout repairs versus Skill-only 8/9. Every Catalog holdout made one exact found lookup and received no runtime note, so the comparison exercises official/abqpy cards rather than memorized per-case patches.",
    "cost_tradeoff": "On ordinary successful trajectories, the 1.7-3.0 KB card often increased input context. Aggregate token use was lower because Catalog avoided one long FieldOutput failure. The observed value is primarily reduced tail risk, not guaranteed per-call token savings.",
    "limits": [
      "Only three API families and three stochastic repeats per arm were used.",
      "The provider exposes no deterministic seed, so attempts are interleaved same-configuration groups rather than identical trajectories.",
      "Two holdout rules are also represented in the Skill; this deliberately tests incremental value over the current Skill, but narrows domain breadth.",
      "These are repair tasks with minimal CAE models, not evidence that Catalog improves every from-scratch simulation task."
    ],
    "product_decision": "Keep Skill as the default compact baseline. Enable exact Catalog for repair/debug tasks, with one qualified lookup per failing API and payload compaction as the next optimization. Keep automatic_verified_only for sparse known runtime incompatibilities."
